Z||000002||Mach10||08-11-2001||02:16 PM||||Glasspack. The straight pipe will resonate and give you a weird buzz at certain revs. Try a Cherry Bomb. I have one, and the low-rev rumble, and high-rev scream is worth it, power gain or not  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000003||crgkstnr||08-11-2001||03:05 PM||||I decided to put on a straight thru steel muffler from Car Quest.  The main body is 5 inches in dia. by 16 inches long.  Part no. EXH 157687.  I had this same muffler on in the past with a good fairly quiet tone.  This time there's a weird buzzing sound.  I think Mach 10 is right about the resonation.  Think I'd better put those little exhaust springs back on.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierohoho||08-11-2001||04:50 PM||||?<P>Are you leaving the stock muffler in place when you pull the cat?<P>I guess I can't understand how it would be louder or does the cat quiet things down that much.<P>Anyone do it to the 4cyl? <P>Results?<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mach10||08-11-2001||05:01 PM||||The Catalytic converter is pretty much a baffle. The gas must touch as much of the elements as possible to work. The interior is like multiple layers of metal screening with stuff on it. It will muffle sound waves, and smooth pulses. Much like a muffler. However, it isn't too efficient as a sound-deadening device, due to the fact that the material is a little too loose, and the fact that a cat is more-or-less a straight-through device. There IS a big difference, though. Not so much in sound volume, which is negligable, but in tone, which is pretty distinct. At least, I can tell the difference  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000006||Fierochic88||08-11-2001||06:50 PM||||Do be careful because in some states (such as PA) removing the catalytic converter is illegal.  There are ways around it though!  I chose to go the straight pipe route so my father and I hollowed out my cat and ran a straight pipe through it.  I'm not sure about increased performance but it sure has a pretty little rumble to it, especially when down shifting!  And...it looks legal to any inquiring minds that peer under the car!<P>Jennifer<P>------------------<BR>IM Username:  FieroChic88<BR>Silver 1988 GT<BR>Silver 1986 2m4<BR>Cobalt Blue 85 GT<P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/jenscar-sig1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Mach10||08-11-2001||07:53 PM||||This is true, you may fail emmissions. However, where I live, it's only illegal if the car's newer than 94. Isn't that the case? I heard it was. I admit that there is no reason for them to be the same but...||||reg||7
Z||000008||artherd||08-12-2001||05:47 AM||||In CA if you breathe on the cat wrong, they will execute you at dawn.<P>PS: just get a modern cat guys, it's got almost no restriction, sounds really mean and exotic, and your car won't stink of fuel. <P>Everyone driving behind you would be greatufl.<P>The stock 1970s tech GM  cats suck(TM)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
